# Add visual goldens and complete keyboard-only editor journeys

## Scope and existing evidence

Introduce reproducible notebook PNG/PDF goldens and the keyboard
navigation/refusal flow for every currently implemented page-editor control.

This is unfinished implementation work. The following original design
records remain accepted theoretical deliverables and are not reopened:

- ATR-P8-003 — archived release design
- ATR-P8-004 — archived release design

## Trusted keyboard-only native profile journey (partial) — 2026-11-03

A real headless Firefox/WebDriver BiDi test uses only trusted Tab, Shift+Tab,
ArrowDown, Enter, Ctrl+A and character keys to edit two live localhost
profiles. It does not invoke DOM click, focus, value assignment or synthetic
input events to make the profile changes. The browser persists a selected
date style, placement and numeric date in a native notebook JSON file and
an exact handwriting seed in a separate native Rust handwriting JSON file.

After unsaved keyboard edits, the test reopens each file and checks the
original values and unchanged disk bytes. Invalid February 30 and a seed
above unsigned 64-bit range trigger visible refusal and preserve both files.

Focused regression:

```sh
node --test \
  --test-name-pattern='Firefox trusted keyboard saves and reopens' \
  tests/browser/workspace/adapter-inbound/runtime-frame-ancestors.mjs
```

This establishes a real keyboard-only save/reopen subset. It does not provide
approved ruled/squared raster goldens or verify every currently implemented
page-editor control, so this record remains open.

## Trusted keyboard bilingual glyph baseline (partial) — 2026-11-04

A second real Firefox/WebDriver BiDi journey exercises the browser's exact
114-key English/Spanish glyph editor without synthetic DOM activation or
pointer input. Trusted Tab, Shift+Tab, Ctrl+A and Enter inspect the next
missing glyph, change geometric input, refuse an out-of-cell circle center
without adding ink, then correct that value and fill the missing baseline.
The live coverage readout reaches exactly 114/114 glyphs, and activating
Fill again leaves the completed draft unchanged.

The same trusted keyboard reaches Download and emits one complete versioned
TSV. A passive download spy verifies that its bytes match the independently
computed 114-key geometric baseline exactly; the action contacts no backend
API and writes no unrequested file. Existing independent real Firefox/native
Rust PDF and transparent PNG tests cover opening and rendering that same
baseline, but this test does not add physical output or claim approved
visual goldens.

Run the focused acceptance:

```sh
node --test \
  --test-name-pattern='Firefox trusted keyboard fills and downloads' \
  tests/browser/workspace/adapter-inbound/runtime-frame-ancestors.mjs
```

The larger requirement to navigate and refuse every editor control using
only keyboard input remains open.

## Disposable Firefox process-group cleanup (partial) — 2026-11-04

The Linux BiDi launcher now tracks each owned detached Firefox group, kills
surviving helpers when the launcher exits, and reaps remaining owned groups
when the test worker exits normally. Bounded shutdown also reaps a group when
a child exit is observable only through its exit code, even if the event was
missed. Linux subprocess fixtures verify surviving helpers are terminated
without signalling a shared process group; eight successive focused runs and
two actual BiDi journeys passed.

`pnpm run test:frontend` now uses one test-file worker at a time so multiple
resource-intensive Firefox journeys do not contend on the workstation.
This is fixture/test-runner reliability evidence, not an approved PNG/PDF
golden or proof of every keyboard-only control. The subsequent
source-authority review restored 70/70 browser privacy and 43/43 native
authority tests, including checks that reject an extra subprocess in the
opt-in print CLI. These audits do not substitute for the still-open visual
goldens and full keyboard journeys.

## Abrupt worker termination fixture cleanup (partial) — 2026-11-04

An independent Linux watchdog now observes each test-owned detached Firefox
fixture over a private pipe. When its test worker is killed with SIGKILL,
the watchdog sees pipe closure and kills only that fixture process group;
normal launcher exit retains the existing cleanup observer.

Isolated Node fixtures test both pipe closure and a real SIGKILL
of the parent worker, including the detached child reaching an
exited or zombie state, without signalling an unrelated browser
session. A real BiDi browser journey remains
necessary alongside these synthetic process fixtures. This does not change
production browser lifecycle guarantees or complete the still-open visual
goldens and keyboard-only control coverage.

## Reproducible ruled/grid candidate image digests (partial) — 2026-11-04

The native paper crate now pins SHA-256 candidate references for A4 ruled
and squared/grid pages at 72 DPI. One Rust regression checks each actual
PDF vector stream, encoded transparent PNG and independently reconstructed
596×842 RGBA pixel buffer. It asserts exact visible blue-grey pixel counts
(34,500 ruled and 66,468 grid), then independently recolors the page to
prove changed ink does not match the saved candidate reference.

The reference hashes were captured from separately invoked, real native
`atrament-paper` CLI PDF/PNG exports; the test calculates digests using the
already-admitted Rust SHA-256 implementation, not a second image renderer.
Run `cargo test --offline -p atrament_notebook_paper_cli --lib
ruled_and_grid_page_candidate_golden_bytes_and_pixels_are_stable`.
All-target Clippy and the full paper test suite pass.

These are **candidate machine-byte and pixel goldens**, not human-reviewed
or owner-approved visual reference images. The test freezes encoding as well
as painted pixels, so intentional renderer/serializer changes will require
an explicit candidate update and visual approval. Keyboard-only coverage
of every editor control and approved image-file goldens remain open.

## Viewable native paper reference candidates — 2026-11-04

The original A4 ruled and grid golden hashes now have checked-in PDF and PNG
reference files in `tests/backend/notebook-paper-cli/composition/`.
They are produced by the actual native `atrament-paper` CLI using
default paper options with only `--style ruled|grid`, and
`--format png --dpi 72` for the raster. Each
596×842 transparent image and one-page vector PDF is independently viewable.

The existing Rust golden test now compares the real renderer's PDF and PNG
bytes against these fixtures as well as the separately pinned SHA-256 hashes,
decoded RGBA digests, and exact painted-pixel counts. A forged fixture cannot
silently become a new baseline just by changing its bytes. The test also
continues to refuse a falsely matching recolored page.

Reproduce with `cargo run --offline -q -p atrament_notebook_paper_cli
--bin atrament-paper -- --style ruled --format png --dpi 72 --output FILE`
(or `--style grid`, or omit `--format png --dpi 72` for the PDF); inspect
the images before any deliberate fixture update. Run
`cargo test --offline -p atrament_notebook_paper_cli --lib
ruled_and_grid_page_candidate_golden_bytes_and_pixels_are_stable`.

These remain candidate visuals, not owner-approved references. They do not
close the required keyboard-only complete editor journey or the final
visual-approval gate.

## Native CLI black-box paper candidate parity — 2026-11-04

The mirrored test `tests/backend/notebook-paper-cli/composition/`
`paper-reference-goldens.mjs` invokes the real Rust `atrament-paper`
binary for both A4 ruled and grid pages, in vector PDF and transparent
72-DPI PNG. It compares all four newly exported files byte-for-byte with
the checked-in viewable candidates and their independently pinned SHA-256
digests, also inspecting physical PDF dimensions and PNG RGBA framing.

Each valid saved output refuses implicit replacement, and an invalid paper
style with explicit replacement authorization still refuses atomically.
This checks the real process and selected output boundary rather than
only calling the in-process renderer. No browser or physical printer runs.
Run the black-box test from the repository root:

```sh
node --test \
  tests/backend/notebook-paper-cli/composition/paper-reference-goldens.mjs
```

These candidates still require owner visual approval.

